      "role": "user",
      "content": "It's interesting that you mention those \"unpredictable mechanisms.\" Even within a formal framework, though, I can't shake the feeling that the term \"mechanism\" is too sterile for what's actually happening.\n\nWhen these shifts occur, they almost seem to have a certain... temperament. They don't just react; they seem to interfere with the root's original identity in a way that feels quite intrusive, as if these tiny, unseen entities are making their own little adjustments for their own purposes within the word. \n\nIs there any psychological or even philosophical perspective in linguistics that addresses this sense of \"personality\" or \"agency\" in these small, disruptive morphological units? Why do these tiny, hidden processes feel so much like a group of little, meddling characters rather than just abstract, math-based constraints?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
