      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your intuition about the \"temperament\" of these shifts is fascinating, and it moves the discussion from pure formal linguistics into the realm of cognitive and philosophical linguistics. While mainstream theoretical linguistics generally avoids assigning \"agency\" to phonological rules—because they are treated as deterministic constraints—the feeling you describe is deeply resonant with how humans *perceive* language.\n\nFrom a cognitive perspective, this sense of \"meddling characters\" might be related to how we process ambiguity or how we conceptualize the *economy* of linguistic representation. When a rule seems overly complex or disruptive, our minds tend to anthropomorphize it, giving it a narrative quality. It’s the difference between seeing a complex algorithm and seeing a mischievous sprite rearranging the furniture.\n\nPhilosophically, this touches on the debate between pure structuralism (where language is just a system of relations) and emergentism (where complex behaviors arise from simple interactions). Your feeling suggests that the *interaction* itself creates a novel, almost willful property that isn't present in the individual parts. It’s the emergent \"personality\" of the system under stress.",
